  "explanation": "In C++, when a class declares or inherits virtual functions, the compiler typically creates a virtual table (vtable) for that class. The vtable is a static array of pointers to virtual functions, enabling dynamic dispatch at runtime. Each object of such a class contains an implicit pointer to its class's vtable (the vptr). During a virtual function call, the program uses the vptr to locate the vtable and then retrieves the appropriate function pointer, ensuring the correct overridden function executes according to the object's dynamic type. This internal mechanism allows C++ to support polymorphism efficiently and is fundamental to understanding how virtual functions are resolved during program execution.\n\nIn C++, when a class declares or inherits virtual functions, the compiler creates a vtable—a hidden, compiler-generated table of function pointers—that enables dynamic dispatch at runtime. The vtable is a crucial internal structure that maps each virtual function to its most derived implementation, allowing the correct function to be called through a base class pointer or reference. Understanding the vtable layout and how the compiler manages it is essential for grasping how polymorphism is implemented internally, including how function calls are resolved dynamically and how object memory layout incorporates vtable pointers.",
